(16227,90745,73984,'No','example@example.com','http://songmakerpl.us','127.239.251.245','EEE','comment','deleted','n','2024-02-28 13:16:03','2024-02-28 13:16:03');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `comments` VALUES
(16112,90320,73286,'Bill Peabody','example@example.com',NULL,'127.93.147.201','Won\'t  many arrest subjects claim they were having a psychotic event?\nHard to prove.','comment','published','n','2024-01-21 19:12:29','2024-01-21 19:12:29'),
(16115,5059,73286,'Waldo Jaquith','example@example.com','https://waldo.jaquith.org/','127.207.162.99','The only reason police would be responding in this scenario is because of the mental health crisis—there would be no underlying crime requiring a police response.','comment','published','n','2024-01-21 19:54:21','2024-01-21 19:54:21'),
(16176,90477,73286,'Danielle Newman','example@example.com',NULL,'127.240.105.244','An individual having a true mental health crisis is easy to prove. It\'s the lack of empathy that causes confusion. Individuals in a mental health crisis should be handled with respect and understanding instead of violence and apathy. Police officers have a duty to de-escalate and establish safety protocol. I believe police officers need more specialized training on interacting with people dealing with a mental health crisis. Also, I would love to see Virginia hire social workers to work alongside officers when responding to mental health crisis calls.','comment','published','n','2024-02-03 00:34:27','2024-02-03 00:34:27');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `comments` VALUES
(16055,90278,73960,'Bryan Miller','example@example.com',NULL,'127.217.112.211','This HB 721 is a bad bill. It is not a bill against rent-gouging, but a bill against raising rent to merely keep up with inflation. That is, a very severe rent control bill. It would authorize local governments to adopt ordinances that limit rent increases, and once adopted, they could only limit increases to less than or equal to inflation (indeed, it would limit rent increases to less than inflation, when the percentage of the Consumer Price Index exceeds a specified level). The ordinance could not even allow larger increases to keep up with inflation or pay for renovations that are needed!\n\nMost economists think rent control is a bad idea: In a 1992 poll, 93 percent of them agreed that rent control reduces the quantity and quality of housing available. As The Wall Street Journal said, \"If there’s any consensus in economics, it’s that rent control achieves the opposite of its intended goal. It leads to housing shortages by discouraging new development and maintenance of existing properties.\"\n\nRent control also reduces housing values -- cutting the tax revenue needed to fund schools -- and cuts new housing construction -- as a study of St. Paul, Minnesota, found. Landlords respond to rent control by cutting renovations and sometimes maintenance.','comment','published','n','2024-01-17 20:00:08','2024-01-17 20:00:08'),
(16146,90412,73960,'Bill Peabody','example@example.com',NULL,'127.97.168.223','Rent control has failed everywhere and always.','comment','published','n','2024-01-28 15:41:27','2024-01-28 15:41:27');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `comments` VALUES
(16142,90396,74616,'William Moncure','example@example.com',NULL,'127.181.193.164','The five needle protocol is absolute pseudoscience. This practice has been thoroughly debunked and ultimately only serves to prey on desperate people who are struggling with withdrawal and mental health concerns. This is often promoted for dealing with addiction and withdrawal, despite the mechanisms of addiction and withdrawal being in the brain, not the ear. Concerningly, the NADA website also says that this process of sticking needles in your ear can be used to treat \"emotional trauma\". This bill seems to not have any requirement that an individual administering this technique have any kind of license or education in Counseling, Psychology, Social Work, or any other relevant field that would allow them to actually be qualified to work with people who are suffering from addictions and trauma. Allowing this treatment is at best taking people\'s money for no benefit, and at worse may lead to cases where people get needles stuck in their ear instead of seeking more effective help. It is also not hard to imagine a situation in which a person has their trauma triggered, or cravings for substances activated, and the unlicensed individual working with them has no idea how to actually handle that situation.\n\nThis approach is snake oil that people want to be able to administer despite their unwillingness to go through education that would actually give them the tools needed to help individuals with trauma history and addictions. Do not legitimize this practice.','comment','published','n','2024-01-26 02:47:33','2024-01-26 02:47:33'),
(16214,90632,74616,'Sarge588','example@example.com',NULL,'127.78.102.190','It seems clear to me in reading William\'s comment that he has not experienced these types of treatments before.  It is not pseudoscience, it has not been debunked, and for those of us who experience the world outside of academics and have actually had the treatments, they work. The danger in judging things without all of the information is that you can truly lead others to create judgements that can limit another person\'s ability to use all tools available to them. No one has stated, nor does this bill state, that this cures all addiction without other elements of treatment, and those that provide the treatment don\'t make those claims either.  My stance is that we should allow all useful tools that may help, not restrict those that are outside of our comfort zone or scope of awareness.  Furthermore, and most importantly, those that speak from a place of fear or judgment likely are speaking from some experience or bias that they bring with them, rather than from a place of open and honest debate. \n\nI would also say that assumptions about the level of training that these practitioners have sounds good until you know that they are behavioral healthcare workers, nurses, nurse practitioners, licensed acupuncturists, and doctors.\n\nOne last point, William may also benefit from doing some real research on snake oil.  I believe he intended to speak about those that sold water that claimed to be the oil of the chinese water snakes, but contained none of the substance.  Snake Oil, in it\'s original form, does have health benefits.  A contradiction at best, and a hurried, ill informed reference at worst.','comment','published','n','2024-02-16 03:08:34','2024-02-16 03:08:34'),
(16215,5059,74616,'Waldo Jaquith','example@example.com','https://waldo.jaquith.org/','127.92.85.120','\"Snake oil actually has health benefits\" is not making the case that you hoped to make here.','comment','published','n','2024-02-16 15:42:14','2024-02-16 15:42:14'),
(16218,90657,74616,'Michael Uppendahl','example@example.com',NULL,'127.2.236.185','There’s actually a lot of evidence that ear acupuncture works, Mr. Moncure’s poorly-informed comments notwithstanding.  I personally have benefited so much from ear acupuncture, that I made the effort to train and become certified in doing it.  My treatments are FREE with donations accepted, primarily to cover supplies costs. I make NO specific claims that it will help any specific person with any specific problem, only observing that many find it helpful, including myself.','comment','published','n','2024-02-19 19:21:09','2024-02-19 19:21:09');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `comments` VALUES
(16039,90257,74045,'Michael A','example@example.com',NULL,'127.179.63.233','This is a bad bill as is any bill or law that attempts to minimize punishment for crimes against persons or property. It sends the wrong message to both the criminal and victim communities. Please withdraw it.','comment','published','n','2024-01-15 20:26:22','2024-01-15 20:26:22'),
(16040,5059,74045,'Waldo Jaquith','example@example.com','https://waldo.jaquith.org/','127.156.149.175','...you object to a bill that shaves <em>one day</em> off sentencing guidelines?','comment','published','n','2024-01-15 20:38:44','2024-01-15 20:38:44'),
(16041,90236,74045,'Bill Peabody','example@example.com',NULL,'127.164.28.54','The purpose of this bill is to circumvent fed immigration laws.','comment','published','n','2024-01-15 21:12:55','2024-01-15 21:12:55'),
(16058,90281,74045,'Lou Di Leonardo','example@example.com',NULL,'127.97.176.161','Where are you, Waldo?  This bill shaves a single day so that illegal aliens will NOT be deported since their punishment was less than a calendar year.  Don\'t be fooled.','comment','published','n','2024-01-17 22:07:02','2024-01-17 22:07:02'),
(16060,5059,74045,'Waldo Jaquith','example@example.com','https://waldo.jaquith.org/','127.72.159.147','For starters, don\'t be an ass.\n\nMichael objected to the bill as \"send[ing] the wrong message to both the criminal and victim communities.\" He didn\'t object to violations of immigration requirements, he said that reducing penalties by <em>one day</em> was unfair to victims. That is absurd.','comment','published','n','2024-01-17 22:15:05','2024-01-17 22:15:05'),
(16084,90248,74045,'Lou Di Leonardo','example@example.com',NULL,'127.236.172.46','Waldo, Waldo, Waldo.  Michael\'s objection is valid but why is it important that he spell out for you exactly what he meant?  He must have erroneously assumed you understood.  But, you didn\'t so I explained the situation for you.  You\'re welcome.','comment','published','n','2024-01-19 19:47:24','2024-01-19 19:47:24'),
(16087,5059,74045,'Waldo Jaquith','example@example.com','https://waldo.jaquith.org/','127.84.75.203','It\'s amazing that you can infer that from his comment that is completely unrelated to your own!','comment','published','n','2024-01-19 20:52:33','2024-01-19 20:52:33'),
(16174,90469,74045,'Norma Margulies','example@example.com',NULL,'127.112.251.97','The intention of this Bill is pretty clear - a vile and cynical maneuver to circumvent fed immigration legislation.  In reality, it is part of a larger strategy undertaken by the Democratic party. We will see how this strategy will play out at the polls in November.','comment','published','n','2024-02-01 20:07:02','2024-02-01 20:07:02');
INSERT IGNORE INTO `comments` VALUES
(16036,90256,73053,'WHP','example@example.com',NULL,'127.14.230.120','This is a good bill, and bills prohibiting mothers boyfriends and other unrelated parties more likely to abuse foster children. More than a year after Grace Packer’s death in 2017, a foster child who once lived with the girl is speaking out, claiming she not only saw her being abused by her adoptive parents but that she also reported it. should also be considered. So taking statements from foster children more regularly should also occur.\n\nA foster child who once lived with a Pennsylvania girl who authorities say was killed and dismembered by her adoptive mother and her boyfriend, told NBC10 she witnessed her being abused years before her murder and even reported it to a foster care agency.  \n\nGrace Packer, 14, was murdered in July of 2016. The girl’s adoptive mother, Sara Packer, and her boyfriend, Jacob Sullivan, were both arrested and charged in the girl’s death. Authorities say the couple killed the girl as part of a rape-murder fantasy they shared and then left her for dead in a hot closet inside a home in Quakertown, Pennsylvania. After several months, the couple allegedly dismembered the girl\'s body and dumped it in a field near Scranton, Pennsylvania where it was eventually discovered in October.\n\nMore than a year after Packer’s death, a foster child who once lived with the girl is speaking out, claiming she not only saw her being abused by her adoptive parents but that she also reported it.\n\n“We weren’t listened to,” the woman, who did not want to be identified, told NBC10. “No one cared to hear what was going on.”\n\nThe woman said she witnessed the abuse when her and Grace Packer were under the care of Sara Packer and her then-husband, David Packer, in 2007. At the time, the couple cared for 30 foster children, including Grace. David Packer was later arrested however and accused of sexually abusing Grace and another foster child.\n\nThe woman who spoke to NBC10 Monday is the third person who claimed they witnessed Grace Packer being abused and reported it to the Impact Project, a foster care agency.\n\n“We told Impact and I told my case workers from Bucks County Children and Youth,” the woman said. “Gracie was hit, slapped around.”','comment','deleted','n','2024-01-15 18:30:14','2024-01-15 18:30:14');
